---
export const prerender = true;
import EditorialArticleLayout from '../../components/EditorialArticleLayout.astro';

const slug = 'remove-ai-writing-tells-before-publishing';
const title = "I Dropped a Useful Article Idea Because I Wouldn't Read It";
const description =
  'I rejected a tidy article that felt dead. This is how I edit AI-assisted drafts for clear facts, real examples, useful links, and a voice I will own.';
const imagePath = '/social/remove-ai-writing-tells-before-publishing.webp';
const socialImagePath = '/social/remove-ai-writing-tells-before-publishing.png';
const imageAlt =
  'Full-body smoke-kawaii girl editing draft cards beside a laptop, source pages, magnifying glass, headphones, red pencil, and a checked browser preview.';
const sources = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content',
    label: 'Google: creating helpful, reliable, people-first content',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content',
    label: 'Google: using generative AI content on a website',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/hardikpandya/stop-slop',
    label: 'Stop Slop official repository',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/woosal1337/blog/tree/b912d5fa59f368253683af2ebfac64ad6d08312d/videos/ep01-the-cure-for-ai-slop',
    label: 'The Cure for AI Slop source project at the reviewed commit',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779',
    label: 'Patterns study: GPT detectors and non-native English writers',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.w3.org/WAI/tips/writing/',
    label: 'W3C: writing for web accessibility',
  },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  {
    href: '/tools/text-to-speech-audiobook-generator/',
    label: 'Text to Speech MP3 Generator',
    description: 'Listen to a draft with a fixed browser voice and download the result as an MP3.',
  },
  {
    href: '/tools/reading-level-checker/',
    label: 'Reading Level Checker',
    description: 'Check sentence length, word difficulty, and reading scores without treating one number as a verdict.',
  },
  {
    href: '/tools/keyword-extractor/',
    label: 'Keyword Extractor',
    description: 'See which words a finished draft actually emphasizes after the writing is complete.',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/free-ai-skills-open-source-tools-organic-growth/',
    label: 'Free AI skills I am testing',
    description: 'See where writing, research, code memory, design, and video tools fit in my broader workflow.',
  },
];
---

<EditorialArticleLayout
  slug={slug}
  title={title}
  seoTitle="How I Edit AI-Assisted Writing Before Publishing"
  description={description}
  label="Owner editing notes"
  breadcrumbLabel="Editing AI-assisted writing before publishing"
  imagePath={imagePath}
  socialImagePath={socialImagePath}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  imageCaption="The smoke-kawaii mascot turns repetitive draft cards into a sourced article, listens to the result, and checks the finished browser page."
  sources={sources}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      I had a useful article on my schedule: how I test hundreds of free tools with Playwright and
      Vitest. The topic was true, and a few developers would have found it useful. I dropped it
      before publication because the version I was shaping felt like internal documentation.
    </p>
    <p>
      I would not have finished reading it. That mattered more than a tidy outline or a clean style
      score. I replaced it with a story about nearly buying a text-to-speech server, getting stuck
      in Microsoft Edge, and making the browser do the expensive job. The technical facts stayed,
      but the second article finally had a decision, a failure, and a result I could defend.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Short answer</h2>
    <p>
      I use AI to sort research, challenge an outline, and catch repeated writing habits. I do not
      ask it to invent my experience. Before I publish, I find the decision I actually made and
      check every claim against a source or a test. I add numbers where they clarify the story,
      read the draft aloud, and cut paragraphs that could belong on anybody's website.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is how I try to make AI-assisted writing sound human. I am not trying to fool a detector.
      I am trying to publish something useful enough to keep my name on it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>The check that mattered was boredom</h2>
    <p>
      The testing article had all the usual signs of a sensible plan. I could explain unit tests,
      browser tests, mobile screenshots, accessibility checks, and how I validate sitemaps. The problem
      was the centre of the story. I had a topic, but I did not have a decision that changed
      anything for the reader.
    </p>
    <p>
      The replacement came from work I had just completed. I had planned a server-backed speech
      system, stopped when the infrastructure became larger than the problem, and tested two models
      in the browser. One uncached Edge run took 70.4 seconds and made a working model look broken.
      That led to visible progress, a smaller fallback model, and a recovery rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those details gave the article a reason to exist. The finished
      <a href="/blog/browser-text-to-speech-kokoro-vs-supertonic/">browser text-to-speech story</a>
      could still explain models and MP3 files, but the reader also saw why I changed the product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>A clean score can still hide flat writing</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://github.com/hardikpandya/stop-slop" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Stop Slop on GitHub</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      My local article gate uses Stop Slop rules to flag filler, repeated structures, vague claims,
      fake drama, and a metronomic sentence rhythm. A strong draft can score 50 out of 50. That
      score means the checker found none of its known patterns. It does not mean the opening has a
      reason to keep reading, the example is true, or the judgment belongs to me.
    </p>
    <p>
      I keep the score because mechanical checks catch mistakes when I am too close to the page.
      I refuse to let the score become the editor. A checklist can spot a familiar phrase. It
      cannot feel bored, question a weak example, or notice that a paragraph has no cost if I
      delete it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>The same fact can sound empty or earned</h2>
    <p>I wrote this deliberately flat version to show the problem:</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Flat version:</strong> Browser text to speech provides a practical way to create audio
      without a separate server. The tool supports several voices and downloadable MP3 files.
    </p>
    <p>Then I replaced the category summary with the part I had observed:</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Version I would publish:</strong> My first uncached Edge run spent 70.4 seconds loading
      Kokoro. The button said Stop, the page looked frozen, and I nearly treated a working model as
      a failed product.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both versions are accurate. The second gives you a test condition, a number, the mistake I
      almost made, and the reason the interface changed. Specific writing is not a pile of extra
      adjectives. Specific writing ties evidence to a decision.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>I do not edit toward an AI detector</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: the <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Patterns study on detector bias</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Search research around this topic quickly drifts into AI detectors and one-click humanizers.
      I understand the attraction. A percentage looks like a clear finish line. Research has also
      shown that detectors can misclassify human writing and can treat non-native English writers
      unfairly. That makes a detector score a poor test of authorship or quality.
    </p>
    <p>
      I do not add mistakes to look more human, swap words until a detector gives up, or promise
      that a rewrite is undetectable. I check whether the article is accurate, clear, specific, and
      honest about how I made it. Those tests improve the page even when no search engine or
      detector ever sees it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>My six editing passes</h2>
    <ol class="editorial-checklist">
      <li><strong>Find the decision:</strong> I name what I chose, rejected, fixed, or learned. A topic by itself is not a story.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the facts:</strong> I open the primary source again and separate measured results from my opinion.</li>
      <li><strong>Add the observed detail:</strong> I use a real input, timing, failure, limit, or result instead of a broad benefit.</li>
      <li><strong>Read it aloud:</strong> I listen for repeated sentence shapes, missing words, and sections that take too long to reach the point.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the mechanical checks:</strong> I use Stop Slop, clarity warnings, reading scores, links, schema, and visual tests as alarms rather than grades.</li>
      <li><strong>Cut the transferable paragraph:</strong> If a paragraph could move to another tool site unchanged, I rewrite it around the actual page or remove it.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Reading aloud catches a different class of problem</h2>
    <p>
      A paragraph can look clean on a screen and sound exhausting through headphones. I use the
      <a href="/tools/text-to-speech-audiobook-generator/">Text to Speech MP3 Generator</a> for a
      listening pass when a draft is long. The browser voice is not judging the writing. It simply
      removes my own delivery, which makes repeated openings and crowded sentences easier to hear.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also check the draft with the <a href="/tools/reading-level-checker/">Reading Level Checker</a>.
      I do not chase the lowest possible score. Technical names sometimes belong in the article.
      The score tells me where to look, and I decide whether the sentence needs a clearer word or a
      better explanation.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tips/writing/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">W3C writing guidance</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      W3C recommends informative headings, meaningful links, and short, clear sentences and
      paragraphs. Those choices help people scan the page and help screen-reader users navigate it.
      Clear writing is part of accessibility, not a trick for making prose look less automated.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>I check the subject after I finish writing</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/keyword-extractor/">Keyword Extractor</a> gives me a blunt summary of the
      words I repeated. If the article claims to be about editing but product names dominate the
      result, I probably wrote a catalogue. If a supporting phrase never appears, I decide
      whether the idea is missing or the phrase never belonged there.
    </p>
    <p>
      I do this late. Writing every paragraph around a keyword list is one of the fastest ways to
      drain the voice from it. Google says generative AI can help with research and structure, while
      publishing many pages without added value can violate its scaled-content policy. The useful
      question is still why the page exists for a reader.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">generative AI content guidance</a>
      and <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">people-first content guidance</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Marks cleanup is housekeeping, not proof</h2>
    <p>
      I also inspect public files that I own for suspicious invisible characters and unnecessary
      provenance metadata. Cleaning a hidden code point or an image metadata field can prevent odd
      copy-and-paste behaviour and keep a file tidy. It cannot prove that a person wrote the words.
    </p>
    <p>
      I keep required authorship and AI-assistance disclosures. I also keep the source links. A
      clean file does not replace either one, and a detector result does not overrule them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>The checklist I use before my name goes on a page</h2>
    <ul class="editorial-checklist">
      <li>Can I point to a decision, test, mistake, or observation that belongs to this article?</li>
      <li>Did I verify every technical claim against a primary source or the working page?</li>
      <li>Did I separate my opinion from a measured result?</li>
      <li>Does the opening give the reader a reason to continue within the first few sentences?</li>
      <li>Would the example still make sense if the reader never clicked a link?</li>
      <li>Did I listen to the awkward section instead of reading it in my own voice again?</li>
      <li>Did I disclose ownership and AI assistance without turning the disclosure into a defence?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      I still use AI skills. My earlier article on
      <a href="/blog/free-ai-skills-open-source-tools-organic-growth/">free AI skills and open-source tools</a>
      explains where they help with research, writing, code memory, and design. The final edit stays
      mine because the judgment has to stay mine.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are editing your own draft, start with the paragraph you feel most tempted to keep. Ask
      what fact, decision, or example would disappear if you deleted it. If the answer is nothing,
      the paragraph has already made the decision for you.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialArticleLayout>
